<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    You cannot teach to mastery without knowing what the pieces are and
    which ones somebody has. That map is the thing schools replace with a
    calendar, and it's the thing a tutor builds in their head over the first
    few sessions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here it's explicit: every subject as a tree of topics, one per learner,
    with each topic's state visible.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">How it's built</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Starts from published standards") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The shape of a subject is not a secret — public curricula describe
        it in detail. So a new subject arrives already broken into topics in
        a sensible order, rather than as an empty page.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Then it's yours") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Add, remove, reorder, ignore. A standard is a starting point, not an
        authority — especially for a household that has deliberately opted
        out of one.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "One tree per learner") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Two children learning the same subject are at different places in
        it, and so is the adult in the house learning it for the first time.
        Nobody is measured against anyone else's position.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Start where you actually are") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A short conversation finds your real edge rather than assuming a
        grade level — which matters most for the adult who is fine at some
        of it and has a hole in the middle.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Green has to be earned</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    A topic doesn't become mastered because somebody ticked it. It moves
    when there's evidence — a retrieval check passed, work produced, a
    conversation that demonstrated it. That's slightly annoying in the
    moment and it's the only thing that makes the map worth believing a
    year later, when you're deciding what to do next or writing a record of
    the year.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Taught through what they like</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    The tutor knows the learner's hobbies, because the app already does.
    Ratios through a recipe, or through fret spacing on a guitar, are the
    same ratios — learned by somebody who is actually paying attention,
    which is most of the battle.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-5">
    <%= link_to "How review keeps it →", about_feature_path("review"), class: "text-primary-700 hover:underline" %>
  </p>
</section>
